      <h1>Withdrawn: “What your security headers actually do”</h1>
      <div class="blog-post-meta">
        <span class="blog-post-date">Withdrawn August 29, 2026</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">correction</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="blog-article-lede">The original article has been removed because it described conditional browser mitigations as though each header prevented an entire attack class.</p>
    <div class="blog-body">
      <h2>What was wrong</h2>
      <p>Security-header behavior depends on the browser, policy details, delivery context, application behavior, and the attacker’s position. CSP, HSTS, framing controls, MIME protections, referrer policy, and isolation headers can reduce specific risks; none is a standalone proof that an application is secure.</p>
      <p>The article used absolutes around <code>unsafe-inline</code>, HSTS, and <code>nosniff</code>, included stale interest-cohort guidance, and offered settings without enough deployment context. Penumbra Forge’s header tools are fixed checklist analyzers. They do not test application behavior or establish exploitability.</p>
      <h2>What would be required to republish it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Current primary browser and standards references for every directive discussed.</li>
        <li>Threat-model-specific examples with documented browser behavior.</li>
        <li>Explicit compatibility, rollout, reporting, caching, and recovery considerations.</li>
        <li>A clear boundary between a header-presence checklist and a security assessment.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Until that evidence-backed rewrite exists, the categorical version should not remain published.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>

